{
  // The serverless handlers under api/. These ship to production as Vercel
  // functions, and until this project existed nothing in the repo typechecked
  // them: tsconfig.app.json includes only "src" and tsconfig.node.json only
  // "vite.config.ts", so `tsc -b` walked straight past api/.
  //
  // Vercel does not read this file. Its Node builder typechecks each function
  // against the nearest tsconfig.json above it, which for api/ is the root one
  // (strictNullChecks off, node16/nodenext module resolution), logs any errors
  // into the build, and deploys anyway. Handler code has to pass under both
  // configs, which is why the handlers narrow with `v.ok === false`.
  //
  // strictNullChecks is ON here and off in the root config, which is
  // deliberate. The validators return a discriminated union
  // (`{ ok: true; data } | { ok: false; error }`), and with strictNullChecks off
  // TypeScript does not narrow it on `!v.ok`. Raising it repo-wide is a larger
  // job and is deliberately not attempted from this file.
  //
  // moduleResolution is "bundler" to match how vitest actually resolves these
  // files. The handlers and api/_lib import with explicit .js extensions, which
  // both modes accept; the tests import without them, which only "bundler"
  // accepts. Tests are never functions (they live under api/_tests/ and
  // api/_lib/), so Vercel's check never sees them.
  "compilerOptions": {
    "lib": ["ES2022"],
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "target": "ES2022",
    "types": ["node"],
    "noEmit": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "strict": false,
    "noImplicitAny": false,
    "strictNullChecks": true,
    "noUnusedLocals": false,
    "noUnusedParameters": false
  },
  "include": ["api"]
}
